.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1225 PM PST Sun Nov 15 2026
(Tuesday through next Saturday)

From the previous forecaster: "A lull in the rainfall arrives
Monday night into Tuesday, with highs remaining cool across the
region. Cold temperature products (Frost Advisories and/or Cold
Weather Advisories) are possible for Tuesday morning, as lows in
the inland valleys drop into the middle 30s to lower 40s, which
could bring dangerous conditions to people living outdoors and
sensitive plants, especially when combined with the recent
rainfall making the body feel cooler.

Another system comes into the region beginning late Tuesday and
continuing into Wednesday, Thursday, and potentially early Friday.
This system is looking to be more impactful than the previous
two. On one hand, the associated low pressure system is expected
to slow down as it approaches California. The frontal rain band
would thus move slower as well, allowing for more rainfall to
accumulate at any particular location. On the other hand, as the
low comes into the state, it brings with it a strong jet at the
500 mb level that`s aimed at the northern half of California.
Model guidance suggests that the jet could bring winds of 60 to 70
knots aloft as it comes across the SF Bay Area and Central Coast.
This would support wind gusts of 30 to 40 mph at the valley
floors and raise the possibility of Wind Advisories for the
coastal, Bayshore, and higher elevation regions. In addition, with
prior rainfall from the systems that came before the midweek
system arrives, flooding concerns will increase as soils saturate
and creeks and streams reach bankfull. This is in addition to a
model trend of remarkably higher rainfall totals across the region
from this system compared to the one we saw last Friday and the
one that`s expected to arrive today. Although there`s still time
for the final rain total to fluctuate, the current forecast
suggests 2 to 3 inches of rain across much of the Bay Area and
Central Coast while the coastal ranges see 3 to 5 inches of rain.
As a final note, the chance for thunderstorms has also continued
to increase and probabilities reach as high as 20-30% during
Wednesday and Thursday. The synoptic setup would favor convective
activity with a cold core low right over or very close to the Bay
Area and Central Coast, robust moisture flow, and the combination
of frontal and terrain-driven lift."

The WPC has added a Slight Risk (at least 15%) of Excessive
Rainfall from 4 AM Wednesday through 4 AM Thursday and a Marginal
Risk (at least 5%) from 4 AM Thursday through 4 AM Friday for much
of the region. This is highlighting the greater potential to see
flooding concerns. An active weather pattern is expected to
persist through the end of the month as the 8-14 CPC`s Outlook
shows that precipitation is forecast to lean above average.

